# 🌐 TryHackMe – OSI Model

## 📌 Overview

The OSI (Open Systems Interconnection) Model is a conceptual framework that explains how data is transmitted and received across computer networks.

It consists of seven layers, with each layer performing a specific function to enable communication between networked devices.

## 🎯 What I Learned

- Understanding the seven layers of the OSI Model.
- Learning how data travels across networks.
- Understanding encapsulation and decapsulation.
- Identifying network protocols and their corresponding layers.
- Understanding the roles of TCP, UDP, IP, and MAC addresses.
- Learning how different network devices operate at different layers.

## 🧠 The Seven Layers of the OSI Model

| Layer | Name | Main Function | Examples |
|---|---|---|---|
| 7 | Application | Provides network services to applications | HTTP, FTP, DNS, SMTP |
| 6 | Presentation | Data formatting, encryption and compression | SSL/TLS, JPEG, ASCII |
| 5 | Session | Establishes and manages communication sessions | RPC, NetBIOS |
| 4 | Transport | End-to-end communication and data delivery | TCP, UDP |
| 3 | Network | Logical addressing and routing | IP, ICMP, OSPF |
| 2 | Data Link | MAC addressing and frame delivery | Ethernet, Wi-Fi |
| 1 | Physical | Transmits raw binary data through physical media | Cables, radio signals |

## 🔍 Layer-by-Layer Explanation

### Layer 1 – Physical
- Transmits raw bits (0s and 1s).
- Deals with cables, connectors, electrical signals and wireless signals.
- Responsible for physical connectivity.

### Layer 2 – Data Link
- Handles communication between devices on the same network segment.
- Uses MAC addresses.
- Responsible for framing and error detection.
- Network Interface Cards (NICs) operate at this layer.

### Layer 3 – Network
- Handles logical addressing and routing.
- Uses IP addresses to identify devices across networks.
- Determines the path packets take to reach their destination.
- Routers primarily operate at this layer.

### Layer 4 – Transport
- Provides end-to-end communication.
- Divides data into smaller units.
- Uses TCP and UDP.

**TCP:**
- Connection-oriented.
- Reliable data delivery.
- Uses a three-way handshake.

**UDP:**
- Connectionless.
- Faster with lower overhead.
- Does not guarantee delivery.

### Layer 5 – Session
- Establishes and manages communication sessions.
- Maintains and terminates connections.
- Helps synchronize communication.

### Layer 6 – Presentation
- Handles data translation and formatting.
- Supports encryption and decryption.
- Performs data compression.

### Layer 7 – Application
- Closest layer to the end user.
- Provides network services to applications.
- Supports protocols such as HTTP, HTTPS, FTP, DNS and SMTP.

## 📦 Encapsulation

Encapsulation is the process of adding protocol-related information to data as it moves down the OSI layers before transmission.

| OSI Layer | Data Unit |
|---|---|
| Layer 7–5 | Data |
| Layer 4 | Segment (TCP) / Datagram (UDP) |
| Layer 3 | Packet |
| Layer 2 | Frame |
| Layer 1 | Bits |

**Decapsulation** is the reverse process, where the receiving device removes the additional information as data moves up the layers.

## 🔐 Practical Learning

- Explored the responsibilities of all seven OSI layers.
- Identified protocols associated with different layers.
- Studied TCP and UDP communication.
- Understood how data is encapsulated into segments, packets, frames and bits.
- Practised identifying OSI layers through the interactive OSI game.

## 💡 Key Takeaway

The OSI Model provides a structured way to understand network communication and troubleshoot connectivity issues. Understanding these seven layers is essential for networking, cybersecurity, penetration testing and network administration.

